records.text:
My father's maiden ground
mightily,
for she saw the doom
of very many men;
great props sprang
from the mill-frame,
iron-bound;
let us grind on further!

"What is wrong with you, Hjálmarr?
You have changed colour;
I say great wounds
are wearing you out;
your helmet is hewn,
and the mail-coat on your side;
now I say your life
has run out."

A raven flies from the east
from a high tree,
after him flies
an eagle in his company;
to that eagle I give
my last flesh;
he will taste
my blood."

No father buried me
nor other kinsmen;
they had Tyrfingr,
the two who lived on;
yet one became
its owner at last."

Angantýr said:

"You shall own it
and enjoy it long;
keep Hjálmarr's bane
hidden;
do not touch the edges,
there is poison in both;
that destroyer of men
is worse than sickness.

A curse is on us, brother,
I have become your slayer;
that will always be remembered;
evil is the judgement of the norns."

"Stand forth
while you ask;
he who answers shall sit."

"Stand forth
while you ask;
he who answers shall sit."

"Þökk will weep
with dry tears
for Baldr's funeral pyre;
alive or dead,
I had no good of the old man's son;
let Hel keep what she has."

"Þökk will weep
with dry tears
for Baldr's funeral pyre;
alive or dead,
I had no good of the old man's son;
let Hel keep what she has."

Wealth nor health
does any man control,
though things go smoothly for him;
many are met by that
which they least expect;
no one himself controls his peace.

Your enemies
never trust,
though they speak fair to you;
promise good;
it is good to have another's
punishment as a warning.

Peace he granted them
with a good heart,
and they promised him gold in return;
they claimed to be reconciled
while they drank together,
but still the falsehoods came out.

Deeds of anger
which you have done,
do not add evil on top of them;
the one you have made weep, soothe
you shall with good things;
that is said to be fitting for the soul.

On God one should call
for good things,
him who has created men;
greatly at a loss is
every man
who late finds the Father.

Asking avails,
especially with care,
for what seems to be lacking;
of everything goes without
he who asks for nothing;
few think of the silent man's need.

Late I came,
early called,
to the judge's doors;
thither I intend to go,
for it was promised me:
he gets the delicacy who demands it.

Sins are the cause
that we go sorrowful
out of the world of terror [?];
no one has fear
unless he does evil;
it is good to be blameless.

Desire and conceit
lure the sons of men,
who are greedy for wealth;
bright coins
turn into long grief;
riches have made a fool of many.

Bent I sat,
long I leaned over,
very eager was I then to live;
but he decided
who was mighty;
forward run the paths of the doomed.

Hel's ropes
came, hard,
bound about my sides;
I wanted to break them,
but they were tough;
it is easy to go when one is free.

The sun I saw
set with blood-red staves;
much was I then leaning out of the world;
mighty it seemed
in many ways
compared with what it was before.

The sun I saw,
seldom more sorrowful;
much was I then leaning out of the world;
my tongue
was turned to wood,
and grown cold on the outside.

Of his own works
each man has the profit;
blessed is he who does good;
away from wealth
was allotted to me
a bed strewn with sand.

All the horrors
you cannot know
which those gone to Hel have;
sweet sins
turn into sore penalties;
ever harm comes after pleasure.

Men I saw then
who had greatly
wasted their flesh with hunger;
God's angels
bowed to all of them;
that is the highest joy.

Högni said:
"Linen cloths lie here | that you heed but little;
they will soon burn, | the cover you saw."
